AWS RDS MySQL Instance Provisioning (devops-rds)
Overview
This README documents the steps followed to provision a private MySQL RDS instance named devops-rds using the AWS CLI for the Nautilus Development Team, fulfilling all criteria including versioning, storage configuration, and autoscaling thresholds in the us-east-1 region.

Requirements Summary
Instance Identifier: devops-rds

Region: us-east-1

Engine: MySQL (Version 8.4.x)

Instance Class: db.t3.micro

Storage Type: General Purpose SSD (gp2)

Allocated Storage: 20 GiB

Storage Autoscaling: Enabled with a Maximum storage threshold of 22 GiB

Step-by-Step Implementation
Step 1: Execute the RDS Creation Command via AWS CLI
Ran the aws rds create-db-instance command in the aws-client terminal specifying all required parameters:

Bash
aws rds create-db-instance \
    --region us-east-1 \
    --db-instance-identifier devops-rds \
    --db-instance-class db.t3.micro \
    --engine mysql \
    --engine-version 8.4 \
    --master-username admin \
    --master-user-password Password123! \
    --allocated-storage 20 \
    --storage-type gp2 \
    --max-allocated-storage 22
Step 2: Monitor Instance Provisioning Status
Polled the instance status using aws rds describe-db-instances until the state transitioned through initialization phases (creating, configuring-enhanced-monitoring) and reached the available state:

Bash
aws rds describe-db-instances \
    --region us-east-1 \
    --db-instance-identifier devops-rds \
    --query "DBInstances[0].DBInstanceStatus" \
    --output text
Step 3: Verification & Completion
Once the status returned available, the setup was successfully finalized and validated for the KodeKloud lab requirements.
